[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I investigated RIP across the core (routers 0–6) and did not find any evidence of current routing instability. On multiple checks over several minutes, RIP-learned routes showed monotonically increasing ages and consistent next-hops, indicating they are not expiring and being relearned. Examples:
- On router0, 192.168.200.0/24 (Location C) via 172.20.1.2, age advanced from 02:35 to 02:54 with no reset; 172.25.1.0/24 and 192.168.15.0/24 behaved similarly.
- On router1, 192.168.200.0/24 via 10.16.1.1 and 192.168.100.0/24 via 10.20.1.2 showed steady timers (no oscillation) across repeated show ip route snapshots.
- On router4 (Location C side), 172.16.10.0/24 (Location A) via 192.168.15.1 and 10.20.1.0/24 via 172.25.1.2 advanced timers consistently without churn.

End-to-end reachability between Location A and C is stable under sustained probing: from pc4 (172.16.10.4, Location A) to pc11 (192.168.200.11, Location C) and back, 30/30 ICMP replies were received with 0% loss in both directions. Earlier “timeouts” during 10- and 20-ping runs were due to the test harness’s 10-second execution cap, not packet loss (confirmed by longer, faster-interval tests succeeding).

Mechanism-wise, if RIP were flapping, we would see route ages frequently resetting to near-zero and intermittent packet loss during sustained pings; neither occurred. Localization and root cause: no unstable interface or daemon behavior observed on any core router. Corrective fix: none required; RIP is currently stable. If reports persist, consider collecting longer RIP logs or link flap counters, but present state is healthy. 
=== END ===